tions. So final applications: [\"halve-a\",\"drop-b\",\"drop-c\",\"halve-a\"].\n\nBut we need to check if passes are applied even if they don't change anything? Usually they are still \"applied\" but result is same. But the name \"applications\" suggests the passes that actually changed something. Because otherwise we would just list all passes each round. But we need to see from fixtures. The three cases have identical inputs? The fixtures show three cases with same payload but different nonce, but output should be same for same inputs? Possibly. There is no variation. So we cannot infer from differences. But the problem likely expects deterministic output.\n\nAnother possibility: The \"applications\" array is the list of pass names that were applied in the first round only? Or maybe the number of times each pass was applied? But schema says array of strings, not counts.\n\nWe need to think about the task name: SE-CR-01 might be \"String Extraction - Character Replacement\"? Or \"String Evaluation - Conditional Replacement\"? There's a common pattern: apply string substitution rules repeatedly, and record which rules fired in order, and final text, and whether a fixed point was reached before max_rounds. That seems plausible.\n\nBut the schema says \"applications\" is array of strings, \"fixed_point\" boolean, \"text\" string. The fixed_point should be true if the process ended because no changes occurred in a round (i.e., reached fixed point), false if it reached max_rounds without fixing. In our simulation, fixed_point = True because we stopped after round3 (no changes). max_rounds=8, so fixed_point true.\n\nNow, need to implement solve(case) that takes payload dict. Must not mutate case. Use only standard library.\n\nImplementation steps:\n- Extract text, passes, max_rounds from case.\n- Initialize current_text = text.\n- Initialize applications = [].\n- For round in range(max_rounds):\n    changed_this_round = False\n    for p in passes:\n        name = p['name']\n        old = p['old']\n        new = p['new']\n        # Replace all non-overlapping occurrences of old with new\n        # Since we need to replace all in one go, we can use str.replace (replaces all non-overlapping)\n        if old in current_text:\n            current_text = current_text.replace(old, new)\n            applications.append(name)\n            changed_this_round = True\n        # else no change\n    if not changed_this_round:\n        fixed_point = True\n        break\nelse: # loop completed without break, i.e., max_rounds reached with changes in each round\n    fixed_point = False\n\n- Return {\"applications\": applications, \"fixed_point\": fixed_point, \"text\": current_text}\n\nBut careful: The break condition: if after a full round no changes, we stop, and fixed_point=True. If after max_rounds we still have changes (i.e., loop didn't break), fixed_point=False.\n\nCheck the round counting: The loop runs rounds from 0 to max_rounds-1. If we break because no changes, we exit early. So the number of rounds executed can be less than max_rounds. That's fine.\n\nNow, need to consider overlapping replacements. For example, \"aa\" replaced with \"a\" in \"aaaa\" yields \"aa\" (non-overlapping). That matches typical replace. So use str.replace.\n\nNow, the output JSON must match the schema exactly. The \"applications\" array may have duplicate names. That's fine.\n\nOne more nuance: The order of passes in each round is as given in the passes list. So we iterate in that order.\n\nNow, we need to output the Python source code as a JSON string. The entire response must be a JSON object with single key \"implementation\". The value is a string containing Python source code. Must escape newlines, quotes, backslashes.\n\nWe'll write the code inside triple quotes or normal string. We'll generate JSON manually.\n\nLet's craft the Python source:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case['text']\n    passes = case['passes']\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\n    current_text = text\n    applications = []\n    fixed_point = False\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\n        changed = False\n        for p in passes:\n            name = p['name']\n            old = p['old']\n            new = p['new']\n            if old in current_text:\n                current_text = current_text.replace(old, new)\n                applications.append(name)\n                changed = True\n        if not changed:\n            fixed_point = True\n            break\n    else:\n        # loop exhausted without break, meaning max_rounds reached with changes each round\n        fixed_point = False\n    return {\n        \"applications\": applications,\n        \"fixed_point\": fixed_point,\n        \"text\": current_text\n    }\n```\n\nNote: The loop else clause executes if loop didn't break. But we set fixed_point to False initially, but if we break we set to True. If loop completes normally, we set to False. So we can simply keep fixed_point default False and only set True when break. But careful: if the loop breaks because no changes, fixed_point=True. If it finishes naturally (max_rounds), fixed_point remains False. That's correct. So we can remove the else and just leave fixed_point initialized to False. But we need to return it.\n\nSimplify: fixed_point = False at start, then if changed == False: fixed_point = True; break. So after loop, fixed_point is as set. Good.\n\nNow, we must ensure we don't mutate case. We are not modifying case, only reading.\n\nNow, we need to output as JSON. The entire response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a string containing the Python code, properly escaped.\n\nWe'll write:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    text = case['text']\\n    passes = case['passes']\\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\\n    current_text = text\\n    applications = []\\n    fixed_point = False\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        changed = False\\n        for p in passes:\\n            name = p['name']\\n            old = p['old']\\n            new = p['new']\\n            if old in current_text:\\n                current_text = current_text.replace(old, new)\\n                applications.append(name)\\n                changed = True\\n        if not changed:\\n            fixed_point = True\\n            break\\n    return {\\n        \\\"applications\\\": applications,\\n        \\\"fixed_point\\\": fixed_point,\\n        \\\"text\\\": current_text\\n    }\"}\n\nBut need to escape double quotes inside the string. Use backslash. Also escape backslashes if any. We'll write the string with proper escaping.\n\nWe can also use single quotes for Python code, but it's inside JSON string delimited by double quotes, so need to escape double quotes that appear in Python code. In the Python code, we have double quotes around dictionary keys like \"applications\". Those will need to be escaped as \\\". Also single quotes are fine. In the Python code, we used single quotes for dict keys like 'text', 'passes', etc. That's fine. But the return dict uses double quotes for keys. So we have to escape those.\n\nAlternatively, we can change the Python code to use single quotes for dictionary keys in the return as well to avoid escaping. That's simpler: return {'applications': applications, 'fixed_point': fixed_point, 'text': current_text}. Then no double quotes inside. But the output schema requirement doesn't enforce the Python code's internal quotes, only that the final returned JSON matches the schema. So it's fine.\n\nThus rewrite Python code with single quotes:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case['text']\n    passes = case['passes']\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\n    current_text = text\n    applications = []\n    fixed_point = False\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\n        changed = False\n        for p in passes:\n            name = p['name']\n            old = p['old']\n            new = p['new']\n            if old in current_text:\n                current_text = current_text.replace(old, new)\n                applications.append(name)\n                changed = True\n        if not changed:\n            fixed_point = True\n            break\n    return {'applications': applications, 'fixed_point': fixed_point, 'text': current_text}\n\nNow, when encoding as JSON string, we need to escape backslashes and newlines. In Python, newlines in a string are represented as \\n. So we must write the code string with literal newlines escaped as \\n. Also single quotes inside the JSON string are fine, no need to escape single quotes in JSON (JSON only requires escaping double quotes). However, the JSON string itself is delimited by double quotes, so any double quote in the code must be escaped. We have none now. But we have backslashes in the code? No. So we can just convert the code to a single line with \\n for line breaks. Or we can keep it as a multiline string in the JSON? JSON does not allow literal newlines inside strings; they must be escaped as \\n. So we need to replace each newline with \\n. And also escape any backslash as \\\\. No backslashes.\n\nWe can also use Python's json.dumps to encode the string, but we are providing the JSON manually. Easiest: write the code as a single line with explicit \\n escapes. But careful with indentation: The code must be valid Python, so indentation matters. We'll preserve indentation by using spaces. In a single line, we need to put \\n and then spaces. That's fine.\n\nLet's construct the escaped string:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    text = case['text']\\n    passes = case['passes']\\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\\n    current_text = text\\n    applications = []\\n    fixed_point = False\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        changed = False\\n        for p in passes:\\n            name = p['name']\\n            old = p['old']\\n            new = p['new']\\n            if old in current_text:\\n                current_text = current_text.replace(old