e same round? Yes, we append each time a pass causes a change within a round. So multiple applications of same pass across rounds produces multiple entries.\n\nPotential alternative: Maybe we should only record the name of passes that change something in each round, but only once per round if multiple changes? The problem says \"each time old is found in text, replace it with new\". That suggests per occurrence? But we are replacing all occurrences at once. Then record the pass name as applied. In some puzzles, you record the name each time a replacement is made, i.e., per replacement? But that would be multiple entries per pass if multiple occurrences. The schema says array of strings, could be many. However, the typical interpretation in such tasks is that you apply the pass once per round, and if it changes at least one occurrence, you record it once for that round. But we appended for each pass that changed something, not per replacement. Our code does per pass per round if change occurs. That seems consistent with \"applications\" as list of passes applied. However, what if a pass has multiple replacements in one round? It still counts as one application? The problem statement: \"each time old is found in text, replace it with new.\" That suggests that each replacement is an individual action? But then you would record the name per replacement. But then the applications array could be large. But the fixture doesn't clarify. The text \"aaaa bb cc\" with \"aa\" -> \"a\": in round1, \"aaaa\" has two non-overlapping \"aa\" occurrences, so two replacements. If we count per replacement, we would append \"halve-a\" twice for that round. But is that intended? Usually in such tasks, you apply the rule to all occurrences at once, and consider it a single application of that rule. But the phrasing \"each time old is found in text, replace it with new\" could be interpreted as a loop: while old in text: replace. But that would be iterative, not simultaneous. For example, with overlapping patterns, iterative could lead to different results. But str.replace does simultaneous non-overlapping replacements. The problem does not specify overlapping behavior. But the example: \"aaaa\" with \"aa\"->\"a\" using str.replace gives \"aa\". If we did iterative: first replace first \"aa\" -> \"a\", then text becomes \"aaa\", then again replace \"aa\" -> \"a\", then text becomes \"aa\", etc. That would likely not terminate? Actually iterative would go: \"aaaa\"-> replace leftmost \"aa\" with \"a\" -> \"aaa\", then again -> \"aa\", then again -> \"a\", and stop. That would yield \"a\". So different. Which is correct? The problem likely expects simultaneous replacement (non-overlapping) because that's more common in such string rewriting. Also the name \"passes\" suggests applying all rules in order each round simultaneously. We'll assume simultaneous non-overlapping replacements. That's what str.replace does. The result from our simulation gave \"aa\" after first round, then \"a\" after second, then fixed. If iterative, it would be \"a\" after first round? Let's simulate iterative for one pass: \"aaaa\" with old \"aa\" new \"a\": iterative replace each occurrence as found (e.g., replace first occurrence, then continue from next char). That would go: \"aaaa\" -> replace first \"aa\" at index0 -> \"aaa\". Then from index1? Usually iterative replace all by scanning left to right, but if you replace, you may create new occurrences. In many string replacement algorithms, you replace all simultaneously to avoid infinite loops. The problem mentions \"each time old is found in text\", which could be interpreted as \"as long as old appears\". But then the process might be: while old in text: text = text.replace(old, new, 1) (replace only one occurrence at a time). But then the order of multiple passes per round becomes tricky because after first pass changes text, second pass might see new occurrences. That could be intended. But the fixture doesn't show exp