lication of the rule? The name \"applications\" ambiguous.\n\nGiven the task is from \"effectslice\" maybe there is a known pattern. However, we must make a decision. Let's re-read: \"Given a text and a list of passes, repeatedly apply the passes in order until either no change occurs in a full round or max_rounds rounds have been executed. Each pass has a name, an old string, and a new string. When a pass is applied, each time old is found in text, replace it with new.\" The phrasing \"each time old is found in text, replace it with new\" could mean that you replace all occurrences simultaneously. Because if you replace one, then the text changes, and then you might find a new occurrence that was not there before, so it's not simply \"each time old is found\" but iterative. The phrase \"each time old is found\" suggests that you look at the current text and replace all occurrences that are present at that moment. That corresponds to a simultaneous replacement of all non-overlapping occurrences. That is the standard interpretation in many such problems (e.g., \"apply this substitution repeatedly until no more changes\"). I think simultaneous is correct.\n\nAlso, the distinction between rounds: you apply all passes sequentially within a round, each using the text after the previous pass. So simultaneous per pass.\n\nThus, we proceed with simultaneous replacement using str.replace.\n\nNow, we need to ensure the output matches schema. The \"applications\" array will contain each pass name that caused at least one replacement in that round. In our simulation, round1: halve-a (caused change), drop-b (caused), drop-c (caused) -> three entries. Round2: halve-a -> one entry. Round3: none. So total 4 entries. That seems reasonable.\n\nNow, we need to output the solved function. We'll produce the JSON as described.\n\nBut also note: The fixture has three identical cases with different nonces. Our code should work for any such payload. Return the same result for same inputs, which is fine.\n\nWe must ensure that we do not rely on any external modules, only standard library. No issues.\n\nNow, let's produce the final JSON. We'll write the code in a single string with escaped newlines. We'll double-check escape characters: In JSON, backslash is \\\\, newline is \\n. We'll do it manually.\n\nThe code string:\n\"def solve(case):\\n    text = case['text']\\n    passes = case['passes']\\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\\n    current_text = text\\n    applications = []\\n    fixed_point = False\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        changed = False\\n        for p in passes:\\n            name = p['name']\\n            old = p['old']\\n            new = p['new']\\n            if old in current_text:\\n                current_text = current_text.replace(old, new)\\n                applications.append(name)\\n                changed = True\\n        if not changed:\\n            fixed_point = True\\n            break\\n    return {'applications': applications, 'fixed_point': fixed_point, 'text': current_text}\"\n\nWe need to ensure that the indentation is correct with spaces. In the above, each level uses 4 spaces. After newline, we have spaces. That's fine.\n\nNow, produce full JSON object. We'll write:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    text = case['text']\\n    passes = case['passes']\\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\\n    current_text = text\\n    applications = []\\n    fixed_point = False\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        changed = False\\n        for p in passes:\\n            name = p['name']\\n            old = p['old']\\n            new = p['new']\\n            if old in current_text:\\n                current_text = current_text.replace(old, new)\\n                applications.append(name)\\n                changed = True\\n        if not changed:\\n            fixed_point = True\\n            break\\n    return {'applications': applications, 'fixed_point': fixed_point, 'text': current_text}\"}\n\n